# Conda and Software Environments

Many bioinformatics tools have complex dependencies and often require different versions of the same libraries. Installing everything into one location can quickly lead to software conflicts.

Conda solves this problem by allowing you to create isolated **environments**, each containing its own software and dependencies.

For example, one project might require Python 3.10 while another requires Python 3.12. Instead of choosing one or the other, you can create separate environments for each project.

---

# What is an environment?

A Conda environment is an isolated workspace containing its own software and libraries.

For example:

```text
base
├── Python 3.12
├── Conda
└── Basic utilities

rnaseq
├── FastQC
├── fastp
├── HISAT2
└── featureCounts

phylogeny
├── IQ-TREE
├── MAFFT
└── trimAl
```

Each environment is independent, so installing or updating software in one environment will not affect the others.

---

# Check whether Conda is available

Before creating an environment, verify that Conda is installed.

```bash
conda --version
```

If Conda is available, you'll see something similar to:

```text
conda 25.5.1
```

If the command is not found, refer to the **Installing Software** guide.

---

# Viewing existing environments

To list all available environments:

```bash
conda env list
```

or

```bash
conda info --envs
```

The currently active environment is marked with an asterisk (`*`).

Example:

```text
# conda environments:

base        *   /home/username/miniconda3
rnaseq          /home/username/miniconda3/envs/rnaseq
```

---

# Creating a new environment

Create a new environment called `rnaseq` containing Python 3.12:

```bash
conda create -n rnaseq python=3.12
```

Conda will display the packages to be installed and ask for confirmation.

Type:

```text
y
```

to continue.

---

# Activating an environment

Before using software installed in an environment, activate it.

```bash
conda activate rnaseq
```

Your terminal prompt will change:

```text
(rnaseq) username@WSP:~$
```

This indicates that the environment is active.

---

# Installing software

Once an environment is active, software can be installed into it.

For example:

```bash
conda install fastqc
```

or

```bash
conda install fastp
```

The software will only be available while the environment is active.

---

# Installing from Bioconda

Many bioinformatics programs are distributed through the **Bioconda** channel.

For example:

```bash
conda install -c bioconda hisat2
```

or

```bash
conda install -c bioconda samtools
```

Bioconda contains thousands of life science packages and is usually the first place to look for bioinformatics software.

---

# Deactivating an environment

When you're finished working, deactivate the environment:

```bash
conda deactivate
```

You'll return to the base environment.

---

# Removing an environment

If you no longer need an environment, it can be removed.

```bash
conda remove --name rnaseq --all
```

This deletes the environment and all software installed within it.

---

# Updating software

Update a package:

```bash
conda update fastqc
```

Update every package in the active environment:

```bash
conda update --all
```

Updating an entire environment should be done with care, as newer package versions may change your results or introduce incompatibilities.

---

# Exporting environments

To make analyses reproducible, export the environment specification:

```bash
conda env export > environment.yml
```

Another user can recreate the same environment with:

```bash
conda env create -f environment.yml
```

This helps ensure analyses can be reproduced on another computer or at a later date.

---

# Good practices

- Create separate environments for different projects or workflows.
- Give environments descriptive names such as `rnaseq`, `assembly`, or `phylogeny`.
- Avoid installing large numbers of unrelated programs into the same environment.
- Export important environments so they can be recreated later.
- Record the environment used for published analyses.

> **Tip**
>
> If you're unsure which environment you're using, look at your terminal prompt. The active environment is shown in parentheses before your username.
